  "text": "Solution 3 is the most likely to be correct, but I could not verify it.\n\n- **Solution 1 (answer 0)** is refuted by its own example. Solution 1 only rules out the Mycielski graph of $C_7$ and then concludes that no graph exists. That does not establish zero, and the problem presupposes that such graphs exist.\n- **Solution 2 (answer 1)** rests on unverified claims. It asserts that the Grötzsch graph has no induced $C_7$ without checking it, and its final step (\"the classification yields exactly one graph\") is asserted rather than derived.\n- **Solution 3 (answer 2)** cites a specific classification (Goedgebeur and Schaudt's work on 4-critical $P_7$-free graphs, plus related results on $\\{P_7,C_3\\}$-free graphs). I recall that this line of work yields a small finite list of 4-vertex-critical $\\{P_7,C_3\\}$-free graphs, with only a couple containing an induced $C_7$, which fits the answer 2. My recall of the exact count is not reliable, and I did not check the source.\n\nCHOICE: 3",
